ese differences across multiple linguistic layers:\n        *   **Syntactic:** How they structure sentences.\n        *   **Lexical:** What words they choose.\n        *   **Semantic:** What they mean.\n*   **Presenter\u0027s Note:** \"이 연구가 단순히 \u0027된다, 안된다\u0027를 넘어, \u0027어떻게\u0027 다른지, 그리고 그 차이가 \u0027어디서\u0027 오는지 밝히는 것이 목표임을 명확히 하세요.\"\n\n---\n\n**Slide 4: Methodology I - Data Collection \u0026 Prompt Design**\n\n*   **Title:** Building the Corpus: Data \u0026 Prompt Strategy\n*   **Key Contents:**\n    *   **Data Sources:**\n        *   **Human:** Real-world answers collected from users on Quora.\n        *   **LLMs:** Generated responses from four major models: Claude, DeepSeek, Gemini, and GPT.\n    *   **Prompt Strategy:**\n        *   To ensure data diversity, we employed **five distinct prompt modes**: Minimal Guidance, Objective, Experience, Analytical, and a combined Core Informative Set.\n        *   This approach allows us to analyze how LLM responses change under different conditions.\n    *   **Scale:** A total of 100 questions were used, sourcing approximately 30 responses per question for each LLM.\n*   **Presenter\u0027s Note:** \"데이터 수집 과정의 체계성과 엄밀함을 강조하세요. 특히 5가지 프롬프트 모드를 사용한 것은 우리 연구가 다양한 상황에서의 LLM 행동을 분석했음을 보여주는 중요한 부분입니다.\"\n\n---\n\n**Slide 5: Methodology II - Feature Extraction**\n\n*   **Title:** A Multi-Faceted Analytical Approach\n*   **Key Contents:**\n    *   We explored a comprehensive set of linguistic features to capture the \"fingerprints\":\n        *   **Stylistic \u0026 Structural Stats:** Basic metrics like word/sentence counts, average lengths, and lexical density.\n        *   **Syntactic Patterns (POS Tagging):** Distribution of parts-of-speech (nouns, verbs, pronouns, etc.).\n        *   **Lexical Signatures (TF-IDF):** Key differentiating terms for each source.\n        *   **Semantic Meaning (SBERT):** Vector representations of text for similarity analysis.\n        *   **Thematic Content (LDA):** Latent topic distributions within the corpus.\n*   **Presenter\u0027s Note:** \"이 슬라이드에서는 단편적인 분석이 아니라, 언어의 여러 차원을 아우르는 \u0027종합적인\u0027 분석을 수행했음을 강조하는 것이 중요합니다. 각 분석 기법의 목적을 간략히 설명해주세요.\"\n\n---\n\n**Slide 6: Methodology III - Classification**\n\n*   **Title:** Putting the Fingerprints to the Test: Classification\n*   **Key Contents:**\n    *   **Model:** A LightGBM multi-class classifier was trained.\n    *   **Task:** To predict the author of a given text (Claude, DeepSeek, Gemini, GPT, or Human).\n    *   **Input Features:** For this specific classification task, we used only the **LDA-based subtopic probability distributions** as input features.\n    *   **Dataset Split:** Train 60% / Test 40%.\n*   **Presenter\u0027s Note:** \"이 분류 모델은 \u0027우리가 찾아낸 언어적 특징들이 실제로 구별 가능한 정보인가?\u0027를 검증하는 핵심 단계입니다. 여기서는 특히 LDA 토픽 분포만으로도 분류가 가능했음을 보여주는 것이 포인트입니다.\"\n\n---\n\n**Slide 7: Results I - Lexical \u0026 Semantic Signatures**\n\n*   **Title:** What They Speak About: Lexical \u0026 Semantic Differences\n*   **Key Contents:**\n    *   **(왼쪽에 TF-IDF 표 삽입)**\n        *   **TF-IDF:** LLMs generate responses using a common, general-purpose lexicon (e.g., \"like,\" \"one,\" \"people\"). In contrast, human choices are more grounded in specific contexts, using words like \"would,\" \"time,\" \"day,\" \"year.\"\n    *   **(오른쪽에 SBERT 히트맵 삽입)**\n        *   **SBERT:** Analysis shows a clear semantic gap between humans and all LLMs. While LLMs are highly similar to each other, human responses are semantically distinct and far more internally diverse.\n*   **Presenter\u0027s Note:** \"두 결과를 나란히 보여주며, \u0027어휘 선택(TF-IDF)과 실제 의미(SBERT) 모두에서 인간과 LLM은 뚜렷한 차이를 보인다\u0027는 점을 강조하세요.\"\n\n---\n\n**Slide 8: Results II - Syntactic Fingerprinting**\n\n*   **Title:** How They Speak: Stylistic \u0026 Syntactic Patterns\n*   **Key Contents:**\n    *   **(포스터의 Box Plot 이미지들 삽입: PRP Proportion, Lexical Density 등)**\n    *   **Key Finding:** Humans exhibit significantly greater stylistic diversity across all metrics compared to LLMs.\n    *   **Human Style:** Characterized by high variability in sentence length, word choice, and POS usage, reflecting individual expression.\n    *   **LLM Style:** More consistent and operate within model-specific patterns. For example, some models prefer longer, more detailed explanations, while others are more concise.\n*   **Presenter\u0027s Note:** \"Box Plot의 \u0027상자 크기\u0027와 \u0027수염 길이\u0027가 바로 \u0027다양성\u0027을 의미한다고 설명해주세요. 인간의 그래프가 다른 LLM에 비해 훨씬 넓게 퍼져있음을 시각적으로 지적하는 것이 효과적입니다.\"\n\n---\n\n**Slide 9: Results III - Classification Performance**\n\n*   **Title:** Can We Spot the Bot? Classification Results\n*   **Key Contents:**\n    *   **(왼쪽에 Classification Report 표, 오른쪽에 Confusion Matrix 삽입)**\n    *   **Overall Accuracy:** The LightGBM model achieved an overall accuracy of **73.6%**.\n    *   **Key Insights from Matrix:**\n        *   Human responses were classified most accurately, demonstrating their unique linguistic fingerprint.\n        *   Claude and DeepSeek were often confused with each other.\n        *   Gemini and GPT had high F1-scores, indicating they have more distinct and recognizable patterns.\n*   **Presenter\u0027s Note:** \"73.6%라는 수치는 \u0027우연을 훨씬 뛰어넘는 수준\u0027으로, 우리 분석이 유의미함을 증명한다고 강조하세요. Confusion Matrix에서 대각선이 얼마나 짙은지, 그리고 어떤 셀에서 실수가 발생하는지를 짚어주면 좋습니다.\"\n\n---\n\n**Slide 10: Discussion - The Nature of the Difference**\n\n*   **Title:** Discussion: Divergence vs. Convergence\n*   **Key Contents:**\n    *   **Human Writing (Divergent):** Characterized by **vast diversity**. This stems from unique personal experiences, memories, emotions, and individual writing habits. The expression is open and unpredictable.\n    *   **LLM Writing (Convergent):** Characterized by **high consistency**. This stems from patterns learned from vast training data and being optimized to produce the most probable, coherent output. The expression is constrained and predictable.\n*   **Presenter\u0027s Note:** \"이 슬라이드에서 연구의 핵심 통찰을 전달합니다. 인간은 \u